// How a FUNCTION-VALUED operand reads in an assertion's `values:` row.
//
// The assertions below FAIL ON PURPOSE, because a passing assertion renders no
// operand at all — so this is the only shape of fixture that can see the row.
// It is also the only way to see the two surfaces disagree: `Debug.inspect(f)`
// is the name-free constant `<function>` and the row carries the NAME, and both
// appear in the report below, a few lines apart.
//
// # The spellings, and why a table of expression spellings cannot cover them
//
// Only two operand EXPRESSIONS determine the function's name statically, so a
// builder that answered from the expression would get the first two rows and
// nothing else. The row carries the name of the runtime VALUE instead, and
// the other rows are the cases that require it:
//
//	double, a module fn ref        <func: double>
//	|x| x + 1                     dropped: rt.RecordOperand suppresses a
//	                              lambda
//	g = double, read through g     <func: double>       NOT <func: g>
//	String.trim, a std method ref  <builtin: strings.String.trim>
//	Token.Computation(double)      Computation(<func: double>)
//	Token.Computation(|x| x + 1)   Computation(<func: <lambda>>)
//
// Row three is the one that rules out reading the ident's own text: the answer is
// the name of the value `g` HOLDS. Row four is a THIRD format under a different
// word, which is why a registry of bare names read through one `<func: %s>`
// would be silently wrong for it, and why the registry stores whole row strings.
// Rows five and six are inside a runtime enum payload, where no node at the
// recording site names the function at all. No spelling table can reach that
// shape, and `08-pattern-matching/variant_payloads_test.nomi` relies on it
// rendering correctly.
//
// The lambda rows are the MECHANISM and not a decoration: `rt.InspectFunc`
// renders an unregistered code pointer as `<func: <lambda>>`, so row six is
// correct precisely because a lambda's pointer is not any registered function's.
// A build that registered lambdas, or that failed to register the std
// thunk, shows up as a wrong string HERE.
fn double(n: Int): Int {
    n * 2
}

fn apply(f: (Int) -> Int, x: Int): Int {
    f(x)
}

fn takes_s(f: (String) -> String, x: String): String {
    f(x)
}

// `Computation` is deliberately NOT the last variant, which avoids a FRONT-END
// defect rather than being a style choice: an enum whose LAST variant has a
// function-typed payload is rejected by analysis with "impl function
// 'inspect': return type Int does not match interface 'Debug' return type
// String", because the synthesized universal `impl Debug` picks up the payload
// function's RETURN type. `enum Token { Eof; Computation (Int) -> Int }` is
// the whole reproduction.
enum Token {
    Eof
    Computation (Int) -> Int
    Word String
}

fn run(t: Token): Int {
    case t {
        Token.Computation(f) -> f(5)
        _ -> 0
    }
}

test "a module function reference names itself in the row" {
    assert apply(double, 7) == 99
}

test "a lambda literal argument is not recorded at all" {
    // rt.RecordOperand drops it; the row that survives is the CALL's, not the
    // lambda's.
    assert apply(|x| x + 1, 7) == 99
}

test "a binding holding a function names the VALUE, not the binding" {
    g = double
    assert apply(g, 7) == 99
}

test "a stdlib method reference renders under builtin, not func" {
    assert takes_s(String.trim, " a ") == "NOPE"
}

test "a function inside a runtime enum payload carries its name" {
    t = Token.Computation(double)
    assert run(t) == 99
}

test "a lambda inside a runtime enum payload is the registry MISS" {
    t = Token.Computation(|x| x + 1)
    assert run(t) == 99
}

// NO `assert Debug.inspect(double) == ...` ROW HERE, and the absence is a
// known limit of how the row is recorded, not a gap in this file's
// coverage.
//
// `Debug.inspect(double)` is `"<function>"`. What the row lacks is the call's
// ARGUMENT: a failing assertion over that call does not record
// `double = <func: double>`, because inspectQualifiedCall answers the call
// before recordCallArgs is reached.
//
// That is general: it is true of `Debug.inspect(<any non-literal>)` inside a
// FAILING assertion, for every receiver this builder renders, and it stays
// out of sight for the reason everything on this surface does: a PASSING
// assertion renders no operand, and no corpus assertion of that shape
// fails. It is not about functions, and a fixture row for it here would
// attribute a general gap to this file rather than to the recording of
// `Debug.inspect` arguments.
//
// The Debug-side constant is not asserted in this file. A passing assertion
// renders no operand, so it would need a failing row, and that row lacks the
// argument for the reason above.
